GUNZIP and GREP Commands – Detailed Writeup
1. GUNZIP Command
What is gunzip?
The 'gunzip' command decompresses files that were compressed using the 'gzip' utility. It restores files from .gz format back to their original state.
Why is gunzip used?
- Used by system administrators to extract compressed logs and backups.
- Saves disk space and speeds up file transfers.
- Makes compressed files readable for processing.
How gunzip works with examples
$ gzip sample.txt
$ gunzip sample.txt.gz
gunzip Output Snapshot
Before:
sample.txt.gz
After:
sample.txt
gunzip Options & Flags
- -c : Write output to standard output.
- -k : Keep compressed file.
- -v : Verbose mode.
- -f : Force overwrite.
- -t : Test compressed file integrity.
 gunzip History
gunzip is part of the gzip utility suite created in 1992 by Jean-loup Gailly and Mark Adler. It replaced older Unix compress utilities and became standard on Linux.
2. GREP Command
 What is grep?
grep stands for Global Regular Expression Print. It is used to search text using patterns.
Why is grep used?
- To search logs for errors.
- To filter command outputs.
- To find patterns in configuration files.
- Supports powerful regular expressions.
 How grep works with examples
$ grep 'error' logfile.txt
$ grep -r 'failed' /var/log
 grep Output Snapshot
error: failed to start service
Found inside logfile.txt
 grep Options & Flags
- -r : Recursive search
- -i : Ignore case
- -n : Show line numbers
- -w : Match whole word
- -l : Show filenames only
- -E : Use extended regex
 grep History
Developed in 1973 by Ken Thompson at Bell Labs. Name comes from the ed editor command 'g/re/p'. It is now one of the most widely used Unix tools.
